        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Deck Staining Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck stain is one of the few home projects where the product matters less than the day you choose to apply it and the state of the wood underneath.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Over an Old Film-Forming Finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new stain cannot penetrate through an existing solid stain or varnish, so it sits on top and peels away with whatever is underneath. If the old finish is film-forming, it has to be stripped or sanded off. Penetrating stains can generally be refreshed over themselves; film finishes cannot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in Direct Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot boards flash the solvent or water out of the stain before it can penetrate, leaving lap marks and a coat that has not really bonded. Follow the shade around the deck, or start early and finish before the sun comes round. The boards should be cool enough to rest a hand on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Maintaining a Wet Edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stopping halfway along a board and picking it up later leaves a permanent dark line where the two applications overlap. Always work the full length of two or three boards at a time, from one end to the other, and never break in the middle of a run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying a Second Coat It Cannot Absorb</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating stains take what the wood will hold and no more. A second coat applied after the first has dried has nowhere to go, so it dries as a sticky surface film that collects dirt and peels. If a second coat is called for, apply it wet-on-wet within the window the label specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Should I use a brush, roller, or sprayer for deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">For most semitransparent stains, a combination of sprayer (for speed) plus back-brushing (to work stain into the grain) produces the best results. A brush alone is best for meticulous application on small decks. A roller works for solid stains but can cause foaming with oil-based penetrating stains. The key is working the stain into the wood rather than leaving it to sit on the surface.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">What's the difference between deck stain and deck sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A deck sealant is typically clear or near-clear and provides water repellency without adding significant color. A deck stain adds color plus protection. Many modern products are 'stain and sealers' — all-in-one formulations that provide both color and waterproofing. For most homeowners, an all-in-one stain-sealer combination is the most practical choice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Ready to Protect Your Deck?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
